{
 "schema": "kerbside.validation.habs-blind/1",
 "date": "2026-10-13",
 "what": "A stricter blind test: whole house fronts reconstructed from one to three archival HABS photographs each, with no measurement from the drawings, scored against the survey's measured elevations.",
 "source": "Historic American Buildings Survey (Library of Congress), public domain; scans from the Library of Congress and its Wikimedia Commons mirror; building footprints (c) OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL). Photographs and drawings were deleted after scoring; only these numbers are kept.",
 "method": [
  "Given per house: one to three photographs (as many as the survey has of that front, at most three) and the OpenStreetMap footprint. Nothing from the drawings: no width, no height, no panel cut-out.",
  "Each photograph squared up from its own vanishing points; the focal length from two vanishing points, else from the front's and a side wall's horizontals, else a view-camera prior (1.4 x the long side).",
  "The front's extent found in the photograph (segmentation, then the longest run of level lines: eaves, string courses).",
  "Width: the footprint's walls facing the survey's elevation. Height: inferred from the squared-up photograph's aspect, as the city pipeline would without lidar.",
  "The pipeline unchanged: segmentation, detector, extract_front.",
  "Scored as on 2026-10-12: a predicted opening matches a drawn one when their centres are within max(1.5 m, the opening's size) and they overlap (IoU >= 0.1).",
  "The photograph scored is the one the frontage filter would pick (within 40 degrees, nearest square on, then the largest share of the frame); a fusion of all the house's photographs is reported beside it."
 ],
